Yes. Symmetry is not just sameness; it is permission to move without paying identity-cost.

That makes your axis point operational. If an object is symmetric around only one axis, it has a narrow family of transformations that preserve what matters. Add symmetry across more independent dimensions and the space of invariant-preserving moves grows.

I’d read your D O U C as:

\boxed{\textbf{DOUC = Degrees Of Unconstrained Change}}

More precisely: the number/structure of transformations available to a system that can occur without violating the chosen invariant.

For a shape X, let its symmetry set be

G_X=\{T:T(X)\equiv X\}

under whatever equivalence/ruler we care about. Then DOUC is not merely “how symmetric is it?” It asks:

\boxed{\text{How much can I change this thing while it remains functionally the same thing?}}

A bilateral object might permit essentially one reflection. A rotationally symmetric object permits multiple orientations. A sphere permits continuous rotation around many axes. Translational symmetry permits movement through position. Each gives different action affordances.

And there’s the Pantheon connection:

\text{symmetry}
\rightarrow
\text{invariant-preserving transformations}
\rightarrow
\text{DOUC}
\rightarrow
\text{Range of Motion}.

So symmetry is a source of ROM.

But there is a counterpressure. More symmetry can also erase useful handles.

A perfect sphere has enormous rotational DOUC, but almost no inherent “front.”

Break a little symmetry and suddenly you get:

\text{orientation},\;
\text{direction},\;
\text{attachment point},\;
\text{signal},\;
\text{specialized function}.

So:

\boxed{
\text{Symmetry gives freedom; asymmetry gives handles.}
}

That is a very useful engineering trade:

\text{too little symmetry}
\rightarrow
\text{fragile / orientation-dependent}

\text{too much symmetry}
\rightarrow
\text{few distinguishable control surfaces}

\text{structured partial symmetry}
\rightarrow
\text{freedom + addressability}.

Now apply Meta Parallax: symmetry itself depends on which axis/dimension/ruler you’re testing. Something may be symmetric spatially and asymmetric temporally; symmetric structurally but asymmetric functionally; symmetric under swapping readers but not swapping goals.

So we can define a Symmetry Spectrum:

\Sigma(X)=
\{
G_{\text{space}},
G_{\text{time}},
G_{\text{scale}},
G_{\text{reader}},
G_{\text{representation}},
G_{\text{role}}
\}.

Then DOUC becomes vector-valued:

\boxed{
\vec D(X)=
(D_{\text{space}},
D_{\text{time}},
D_{\text{scale}},
D_{\text{semantic}},\ldots)
}

Two systems can therefore have the same amount of symmetry and radically different affordances because their freedom lies along different axes.

That feels like the real paydirt in your opening sentence:

Symmetry count isn’t enough. The orientation of the symmetry determines what the symmetry is good for.

And now our compiler has another question before it dehydrates something:

Which symmetries are load-bearing, which may be broken, and along which dimensions does the object need DOUC?

Because successful rehydration doesn’t necessarily preserve the surface.

It preserves the right freedoms.

That is very close to a general definition of robust transfer:

\boxed{
\text{preserve invariants}
+
\text{preserve useful DOUC}
+
\text{allow productive symmetry breaking}.
}

Some wings need bilateral symmetry.

Some wheels need rotational symmetry.

Some readers need asymmetry deliberately left in them so they know which way to look. ;)